@100 = ~Hannibal the Pirate
Delgado, a frightened debtor in River District, told me that Hannibal is a powerful pirate who holds sway over the district and works alongside the Shadow Thieves. Delgado says the store north of his house is a cover for the pirate's operations. There may be a concealed entrance behind the store, up the stairs. If we mean to challenge Hannibal, that might be our way in.~
@101 = ~Wife or Slave
Larissa wants two magical potions from a brewer somewhere in the forests east of Athkatla: one to suppress her husband's desire for other women and another to force him to love her again. She gave us 120 gold pieces toward their purchase. No location has been marked yet, so we'll need to find this brewer later.~
@200 = ~Basilisk inside a shanty like this? It must have been imported here or wandered in through the sewers. Let us check this place carefully to ensure that was the only one.~
@201 = ~How could this creature get into someone's home? I see the guards do not exaggerate when they say how poorly scrutinised this place is, if creatures like this are nesting inside people's houses.~

// v2.7 short, overhead-only basilisk comments, split for readability.
@202 = ~Basilisk inside a shanty like this? It must've been imported here or wandered in through the sewers.~
@203 = ~Let's check this place carefully to ensure this was the only one.~
@204 = ~How could this creature get into someone's home?~
@205 = ~I see guards don't exaggerate saying how under-scrutinised this place is if creatures such as this are nesting inside people's houses.~

@102 = ~I delivered both special potions to Larissa. She hopes they will restore her marriage, though whether they truly can remains uncertain. She gave us 200 gold pieces and Defender of Amn, the shield her father carried in military service. My part in her request is complete.~

@104 = ~Hannibal the Pirate
Hannibal is dead. I told Delgado, who thanked us for freeing the River District from the pirate and his brigands. His debt to Hannibal can no longer be enforced; this matter is settled.~
@206 = ~I've heard the news about what you did. You delivered the finishing blow to Hannibal. The whole city owes you a lot.~
@207 = ~Hannibal is gone. You saved far more than just me from his thugs and his debts. Thank you again.~
@208 = ~Oh... I-I-I haven't got the money yet... you came too early...~
@209 = ~Hold on. What are you talking about?~
@210 = ~Don't worry. I am not here for any money.~
@211 = ~What... I mean, give us the money you are due.~
@212 = ~I cannot thank you enough for helping me with Hannibal's debt. You have already given me more than I could ever have hoped for.~
@213 = ~The entrance to Hannibal's compound is behind the store north of here, up the stairs. Please be careful.~
@214 = ~I'm sorry, but I have nothing more to say. I have to find some way to settle my debts.~
@215 = ~I have to pay Hannibal back, and there is precious little time. I don't know what else to do.~
@216 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
@217 = ~Y... you're not sent by Hannibal? To collect my debt?~
@218 = ~No, we aren't.~
@219 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
@220 = ~You sure look different. I... I thought Hannibal sent you to collect the money from me. My debt.~
@221 = ~No, he hadn't.~
@222 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
@223 = ~I-I-I only got 80 coins. I can get the remaining 520, I promise. I-I just need more t-time.~
@224 = ~No. Your time has come to an end.~
@225 = ~Woah, we were just joking. We didn't come here for any money.~
@226 = ~G-gods! No...!~
@227 = ~Oh, what a great joke! Seriously?!~
@228 = ~I found it awfully unfunny. Now that you've had your laugh, can you vacate my home? I have to pay Hannibal back my debt, and I have very little time.~
@229 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
@230 = ~Oh my... I almost gave you my money. That mistake would cost me my life. Thank you for your honesty, though I doubt that will save me in the end. Hannibal always gets what he wants.~
@231 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
@232 = ~I assume you're a stranger to Athkatla if you don't recognise the name. Hannibal is a lowlife pirate who practically owns half the underground of Athkatla. He controls the money, opium, women, trade and more.~
@233 = ~He walks in lockstep with the Shadow Thieves, that much is certain. Little as I know of their dark dealings, I can tell you this: it is no mere master-and-servant affair, but a partnership born of mutual respect. In fact, word on the cobbles is that the Shadow Thieves themselves tread lightly around Hannibal. They show him fear and loyalty.~
@234 = ~The River District? It's a right cesspool, worse than any slums. We're dirt poor here, living hand to mouth. I was desperate for coin, so I went to one of Hannibal's men—a dirty, crooked bloodsucker of a money-lender. I borrowed 550 gold pieces just to keep a roof over my head, and the bastard only gave me a week to pay it back with interest. 600 coins. I don't have that kind of money!~
@235 = ~I am ruined! Utterly ruined. I won't pay it back. I was given an additional day. Could you believe it? A day! I can't involve the guards, or leave town, or pretty much do anything. I'm stuck in this crisis.~
@236 = ~Well... since you've been kind enough to listen to my story, maybe you could help? Look, I won't blame you if you cannot pay for me. You hardly know me, so if this is below you, maybe we can work something else out?~
@237 = ~If this Hannibal spreads panic and terror, and the law is clearly inconsiderate toward him, maybe the best course is to take the offensive.~
@238 = ~I will give you 600 gold coins to pay your debt.~
@239 = ~I would give you 600 gold coins, but I don't have enough.~
@240 = ~I feel really bad for you. Living in this dump is one thing, but being on the brink of homelessness is another. I will give you 2000 gold coins to cover your debt, housing and a fresh start.~
@241 = ~I am sorry, but I can't dawdle away my time and money for this. You're on your own.~
@242 = ~I understand. These aren't easy times for any of us.~
@243 = ~Offense? Heading inside their headquarters is suicide. Hannibal has people everywhere in his underground compound. He is well protected by mages, warriors and Helm knows what else.~
@244 = ~You seem confident in your abilities. As marvellous as that sounds, I am not certain you'll get past his defences unharmed. I can of course point you in the right direction. In order to enter his compound, find the store north of here. The store is a cover for their operation. If you go around the back, up the stairs, you'll find a hidden door leading straight inside.~
@245 = ~How do you know this?~
@246 = ~I lived here my whole life. Little escapes my notice. Over a decade ago, an entrance to the sewer stood exactly where the store is now. Everyone in the area knows this. Hannibal isn't trying to hide his lair. He's too proud to fear opposition.~
@247 = ~You'd be doing the whole city a huge favour if you manage to defeat him. May Chauntea be with you.~
@248 = ~You will?! That is... unexpected. I thank you. I cannot believe this! I wish I could... pay you back, alas I... cannot. It will never be forgotten!~
@249 = ~You're giving me... 2000 gold coins?! You are positively insane! I could never dream of such a sum. This could pay off my debt and much more. I cannot give you anything in return. Whenever someone asks, I will do what it takes to persuade them of your kindness. I am forever in your debt.~
@250 = ~Thank you again. May good fortune follow you.~
@251 = ~No need to apologise. I understand your decision. We live in tough times and money is hard to come by. I'm sorry to trouble you.~
@252 = ~Nate! The owners are back!~
@253 = ~What d'ya think, idiot?! Kill 'em!~
@254 = ~Nate... you're killin' me. I'm yer brother, and ye do as me say. We be rich, ye?~
@255 = ~Ye! That's the spirit. Terribly sorry, but ye're at the end of yer rope, folk!~
@256 = ~Wh... what?! What do we do?!~
@257 = ~But... I've never killed before. Isn't that... a morally bad thing to do?~
@258 = ~And maybe all our faults at the guild will be forgiven. Right?~
@259 = ~By Ilmater's tears! You're not here to rob me, are you? I've got no gold!~
@260 = ~Well? What do you want?~
@261 = ~What are you, Aerie? My mother?~
@262 = ~You've nothing worth taking.~
@263 = ~I am sorry for barging into your place.~
@264 = ~Just be out of here.~
@265 = ~Agreed. Now, you remember the way out, I take it.~
@266 = ~(Sigh.) Apology accepted. Just... don't make a mess and leave.~
@267 = ~Argh... w-wait... w-what? What is going on?~
@268 = ~You've already saved me. I will never forget it.~
@269 = ~My head is still spinning. What happened?~
@270 = ~You were petrified by a basilisk. We've brought you back to your human form.~
@271 = ~We saved you from eternity in stone, and now we expect payment.~
@272 = ~I... I remember. I was about to take my laundry out and noticed the door was slightly ajar. Next thing I knew, this lizard creature came in as if it owned the place. I tried to shoo it away with a chair and... then I saw you.~
@273 = ~Hells, I would have remained like that forever. I don't know how I could possibly repay you. Please, take this as a token of my gratitude. Thanks so much.~
@274 = ~So you killed that thing? Ehm, I have very little money to give you. You wouldn't be satisfied with the amount, but... take this ring I bought. It should suffice. It's magical, but you can sell it if you don't want it.~
@275 = ~Leave me be. You've had your chance to help me, and you failed.~
@276 = ~Thank you again. I can only hope the potions will work.~
@277 = ~Have you found those two potions yet? I have precious little hope left.~
@278 = ~Yes. I've brought both potions.~
@279 = ~No. Not yet.~
@280 = ~Then get to it!~
@281 = ~Yes! Amazing! I only hope it will work. I'll give you 200 gold coins for your expenses and this shield. It belonged to my father during his years of service in the military. It'll prove more useful to you than to me.~
@282 = ~What do you want?~
@283 = ~Why are you crying?~
@284 = ~We're here to rob you.~
@285 = ~Nothing. Wrong house.~
@286 = ~My husband... after work he always goes to see another woman. And she isn't even the only one he visits.~
@287 = ~He is a handsome, eye-pleasing man, and he knows it well. My feelings mean nothing to him anymore. I am nothing more than a mere possession to him now.~
@288 = ~I scarcely know what to do with myself.~
@289 = ~Could I help you somehow?~
@290 = ~You should have chosen a more worthy man.~
@291 = ~I'm sorry, but I am terrible with love conflicts.~
@292 = ~Too late. All I want is to be happy, and for that to happen I need to make my beloved love me as he did before.~
@293 = ~As a matter of fact, yes. I came up with an idea. I heard that a brewer living somewhere in the forests east of Athkatla mixes magical ingredients into special potions. Their price is steep, but I am willing to part with my money for my marriage's sake. One potion makes men lose their appetite for women. The second works like a charm—literally. He will see me as he did when we first met.~
@294 = ~If you bring me those potions, I will give you my father's shield. He carried it during his years of military service. It is of far more use to an adventurer than to me.~
@295 = ~I've made up my mind. So what do you think? Will you visit the brewery for me?~
@296 = ~I'll see what I can do.~
@297 = ~I am sorry, but I have more important things to do.~
@298 = ~You and I both. Eh... fine, get out of my house then!~
@299 = ~Splendid. When it's done, come back to me. Here is 120 gold coins. It should be enough for both potions.~
@300 = ~Like what? Eh, you know what? Good. Don't bother.~
@301 = ~Just go, then.~
@302 = ~Aaaaarh!~
@303 = ~Welcome to Baldie's Goods. What can I get ya?~
@304 = ~Show us what you have.~
@305 = ~Are you comfortable with your shop being in a literal shantytown?~
@306 = ~I'll be going.~
@307 = ~Comfortable? Not in the least, but we live in tough times and I cannot complain. Beggars can't be choosers, as they say. Before this building was erected, a sewer entrance stood directly here, until the place was repurposed into a store.~
@308 = ~Juvenile attempt at a jest, <CHARNAME>. My support requires a level of maturity that I can only hope you provide.~
@309 = ~Hannibal—his name sends shivers down the spine of anyone who has come across it. A pirate living beyond his means and a big roughneck around here.~
@310 = ~Such is life if you choose the wrong partner for the love of your life. I understand your misery, but you need to stay strong.~
@311 = ~As tempting as it must be for you, it is also unwise. Some men are selfish and degrading, but making them your love slaves isn't the same as fixing your marriage. You should consider that thoroughly.~
@312 = ~I applaud your decision. As much of a swine as her husband must be, turning him into an obedient, loving husband against his will is cruel and vile.~
@313 = ~Can't you see this man is trembling with fear? Have some shame, <CHARNAME>. This isn't the time for jests.~
@314 = ~Fool is afraid of his own shadow. Perhaps pressing him for some financial gain for our cause wouldn't be irrational.~
@315 = ~You've been turned into rock by a giant lizard. We should demand a proper reward from this imbecile. After all, we wasted our precious time saving him.~
@316 = ~This joke was truly mean, <CHARNAME>. I hope you think twice next time.~
@317 = ~I'm seriously worried about the direction you take us, <CHARNAME>. Breaking into other people's houses? Who does that?~
@318 = ~I... don't even know how to respond to that. Just... b-be less carefree, all right?~
@319 = ~Does corruption have to be everywhere in this city? It is stomach-turning to the core.~
@320 = ~You've made a wise choice, <CHARNAME>. We saved this man from a grim fate. Regardless, I would take a closer look at Hannibal and his illegal operations.~
@321 = ~And instead of taking your business someplace else, you stayed in this wet dump. A man of peculiar intelligence.~
@322 = ~Oh, what a great joke! Seriously?!~
@323 = ~Juvenile attempt at a jest, <CHARNAME>. My support requires a level of maturity that I can only hope you provide.~
@324 = ~Can't you see this man is trembling with fear? Have some shame, <CHARNAME>. This isn't the time for jests.~
@325 = ~Fool is afraid of his own shadow. Perhaps pressing him for some financial gain for our cause wouldn't be irrational.~
@326 = ~This joke was truly mean, <CHARNAME>. I hope you think twice next time.~
@327 = ~I found it awfully unfunny. Now that you've had your laugh, can you vacate my home? I have to pay Hannibal back my debt, and I have very little time.~
@328 = ~Who is Hannibal?~
